# Synopsis

Manage the tool sets of the current session, retrieve the full signature
of a tool, or explore the tool tree and tool documents.

# Tool sets

The tools of a session form three sets:

- The default tool set is loaded with full signatures when the session
  starts.
- The extra tool set is loaded with stub signatures; call "describe" to
  retrieve the full signature of one of its tools before calling it.
- The enabled tool set is the union of the two, and is what the model
  can see in the session. This tool can also list tools outside the
  enabled tool set.

# Operations

- "describe": return the full signature (description and parameters) of
  the tools of the extra tool set matching "pattern"; with no "pattern",
  nothing is described. Tools already described in this session are
  skipped.
- "list": list the tools of the extra tool set matching "pattern" (all
  of them when "pattern" is omitted); set "ignore_extra_set" to list all
  tools instead. "with_summary" adds each tool's short summary;
  "start_index" and "count" select a page of the result.
- "doc": read documents of a tool. Requires "toolname"; "docpaths"
  selects the documents to read (defaults to "README.md"; several
  documents can be read in one call). Documents that do not exist are
  silently omitted from the result.
- "listdoc": list the documents of a tool. Requires "toolname";
  "pattern" can be used to filter by document path globs.

# Patterns

"pattern" is a list of space separated glob patterns. The matching target
depends on the operation: for describe and list, patterns without "/"
match tool names while patterns containing "/" match paths in the tool
tree (e.g. "builtin/*"), for listing items under the root of the tool
tree, prefix your pattern with a "/" (e.g. "/*"); for listdoc it matches
document pathnames under the tool's doc directory. A word starting with
"@" names a tool preset (a named list of patterns, e.g. "@default"); it
is expanded in place and can be mixed with other words.

# Tool tree

The tool tree is a classification tree over the available tools: directories
are categories and files are concrete tools (named after the tools). The
same tool may appear under multiple categories; each position is listed
separately.

# Tool documents

Tool documents are plain-text (usually Markdown) files under the tool's
doc directory; they hold extended reference material for the model.
"README.md" is the conventional entry point.

# Returns

A JSON object. On success:
describe returns {"described":...,"tools":[{...}, ...]}; list returns
{"list":[{"name" or "path":..., "summary"?:...},...],"total":...,
"start_index":...,"count":...}; doc returns
{"toolname":...,"docs":[{"docpath":...,"content":...},...]}; listdoc returns
{"toolname":...,"doclist":[...]}.
On failure: {"error": <reason>}; when rejected: {"rejected": <reason>}.

# Notes

- A tool of the extra tool set can be described once per session; later
  "describe" calls skip it.
- The tool sets are scoped to the current session.
- Use this tool to inspect what is available and "describe" to learn how
  to call a tool; use the shell tool to read other files.
